WHY THIS IS NOT AN OPEN-SOURCE LICENCE, since a proprietary notice on a public
repository is unusual enough to deserve a reason rather than a lawyer's silence:

This code drives AUTHENTICATED SESSIONS against a third-party job platform whose
terms of service restrict automated access. That is a considered risk taken by
one person, on his own account, with his own data, in full knowledge of what he
is agreeing to. A permissive licence would invite strangers to point the same
automation at their own accounts on the strength of a stranger's judgement, with
this repository's name attached to whatever followed. Nobody should inherit that
decision by default, and an MIT header is exactly how that inheritance happens
quietly.

So: read it, learn from it, argue with it, quote it in a review. Do not deploy
it. If you want to do something with it that this notice forbids, ask.

